narrative:
The Sun Temple is gone so completely that the sky appears indecent above its foundations. Two mourners stand where the altar was. One wants every stone restored; the other says no god worth keeping would ask for a copy.

@Bellik Thorne: You cannot rebuild what was betrayed.
@Ash-Speaker: Then we should not pretend we can. We should learn what the ash remembers.

Ithrenel Dusk arrives after sunset and touches none of the rubble.

@Ithrenel: The Temple was not built to make the sun kinder. It was built to keep a door from opening when people became desperate enough to knock.

Relic-Keeper Sorne watches you from the broken stair. @Sorne: Which part of that story do you intend to use?

By the next market day, grief has acquired pamphlets. The Ash-Speaker asks people to admit the temple failed them. Bellik asks them to name the people who made it fail. Inquisitor Halvard Rane has begun attending both gatherings, smiling at neither.

@Ash-Speaker: A story can be a shelter.
@Bellik: Or a weapon, if cowards are holding it.
